diagnosis_text:
The helpdesk complaint describes intermittent multi-second network pauses simultaneously affecting PC1 and PC2 on the 10.5.0.x floor (gateway 10.5.0.254 on sshswitch). A complete Phase 1–4 diagnostic pass was performed and no fault was found.

**Affected component:** No subsystem is faulty. The gateway br_v5 (10.5.0.254) on sshswitch is up and responding. The VLAN 5 access path (PC1/PC2 → switch4 → sshswitch → router17) is fully operational.

**Mechanism:** The hard gate for intermittent claims requires a confirmed mechanism and historical trace before a fault can be declared. None exists here: (1) pressure_sweep found no flap script or stress tool on any of the 28 devices; (2) sshswitch's kernel ring buffer (dmesg) contains only boot-time bridge initialization events — no post-startup link-down, carrier-change, or bond-failover events; (3) bond0 on both sshswitch and router17 reports link_failure_count = 0 for every slave; (4) FRR/OSPF logs are empty and journalctl has "No entries"; (5) tc_snapshot and a direct tc qdisc check confirm no shaping or netem delay anywhere including on sshswitch.

**Localization:** No device or interface is implicated. All 27 measured ping paths show 0 % loss. OSPF is fully converged across all 9 routers with 18 routes each.

**Root cause:** The reported symptom cannot be confirmed — there is no mechanism (no flap script, no STP, no link failure, no tc shaping, no ACL) and no historical trace (carrier counters at baseline, logs empty) to substantiate intermittent pauses.

**Fix:** No corrective action is warranted. If the symptom recurs, capture a concurrent ping trace from PC1/PC2 during a pause event to provide direct evidence of the failing path.
=== END ===